  1	# Streamable learning interfaces
  2	
  3	Keating treats understanding as the scarce resource. The interface should help a learner inspect, manipulate, resume, and discuss an explanation instead of turning every teaching move into an opaque tool call.
  4	
  5	## Runtime shape
  6	
  7	```text
  8	learner request
  9	  -> session-start hooks add learner, review, goal, and capability context
 10	  -> agent teaches in Markdown and optionally emits a typed OpenUI document
 11	  -> web renders the document incrementally and routes actions back to the agent
 12	  -> lifecycle events record evidence without requiring model calls
 13	
 14	optional work
 15	  -> activate_capabilities loads one or more schema bundles
 16	  -> consolidated operations dispatch to the existing deterministic implementations
 17	```
 18	
 19	The deterministic pedagogy engine remains below both user interfaces. OpenUI is a presentation protocol, not a replacement for learner state, grading, artifact generation, or policy safeguards.
 20	
 21	## OpenUI document contract
 22	
 23	The web agent receives the generated grammar for Keating's curated component library in its system prompt. It may mix ordinary Markdown with fenced `openui` programs. A fence carries one authoritative document envelope:
 24	
 25	````text
 26	```openui lifecycle=resumable id=fractions-check-1
 27	root = LearningSurface({ ... })
 28	```
 29	````
 30	
 31	Lifecycle meanings:
 32	
 33	- `ephemeral`: useful only in the current teaching moment; state is message-local.
 34	- `resumable`: an unfinished interaction that should survive leaving and returning to a session.
 35	- `workspace`: a learner-owned artifact intended for longer-term inspection and reuse.
 36	
 37	The initial renderer persists resumable and workspace state locally. Moving those two lifecycles to the session store and artifact store respectively is the next persistence migration; the envelope already keeps that change independent of the model grammar.
 38	
 39	Legacy `<keating-*>` transcripts remain readable. New model-generated interactions should use OpenUI, while existing deterministic tools may continue producing legacy artifacts until their storage and grading contracts have migrated.
 40	
 41	## Capability bundles and hooks
 42	
 43	Baseline teaching keeps only frequently useful, low-risk schemas. Optional capabilities are discoverable in the session context and activated together with one `activate_capabilities` call:
 44	
 45	| Bundle | Public operations | Purpose |
 46	| --- | --- | --- |
 47	| Media | `animate`, `generate_image` | Visual explanations and authored animations |
 48	| Workspace | `workspace_inspect`, `workspace_change`, `workspace_exec` | Batched inspection, transactional changes, and execution |
 49	| Improvement | `evaluate_teaching`, `request_teaching_improvement` | Evidence review and safeguarded evolution |
 50	| Voice | `keating_voice` | Speech when the configured runtime enables it |
 51	
 52	Hooks own deterministic reactions that should not consume an agent turn: session hydration, interaction evidence, persistence, and idle notifications. Tools remain appropriate when the model must choose an operation, supply authored content, cross a permission boundary, or consume a result.
 53	
 54	The consolidated operations are the model-facing API. The older fine-grained tools remain internal adapters during migration so their tested implementations and artifact formats are not duplicated.
 55	
 56	## OpenTUI boundary
 57	
 58	`keating tui` is a separate OpenTUI host connected to the same Pi runtime through RPC. `keating shell` is unchanged, so existing Pi prompts, skills, extensions, sessions, provider configuration, and direct Pi workflows remain portable.
 59	
 60	The alternate host currently supports the shared transcript, streaming responses, prompts and follow-ups, notifications, status, and editor-text requests. `/shell` switches into the classic Pi interface. Pi-specific modal extension requests are cancelled with an explicit switch notice because silently approximating them could lose information.
 61	
 62	This makes the sacrifice visible: OpenTUI does not yet have semantic renderers for the web OpenUI document model or every Pi extension UI primitive. Reaching parity requires terminal renderers and lifecycle storage adapters, not a fork of the pedagogy engine. Until then, the classic shell is the compatibility surface and the web app is the complete component surface.
 63	
 64	## Migration constraints
 65	
 66	1. Keep lifecycle metadata at the document envelope and give every resumable or workspace document a stable ID.
 67	2. Route interaction commits through typed host callbacks and lifecycle events; do not add global browser events.
 68	3. Promote at most one active assessment into a focused surface at a time.
 69	4. Preserve historical transcript rendering throughout the migration.
 70	5. Store resumable state with its session and workspace state with exported artifacts before removing the local persistence adapter.
 71	6. Build terminal components against the same semantic documents before claiming web/OpenTUI parity.
